 Power BI Advanced – Security (RLS & Azure AD Integration)
TASK 1 — Implement Static Row-Level Security (RLS)
Objective:
Create RLS roles that restrict data for specific users based on region.

Step 1 — Open Model View
Left panel → Model View

Select table:

SalesTable
Step 2 — Create RLS Role
Modeling → Manage Roles → Create:

Role Name: North_Region
Add filter:

Column:

SalesTable[Region]
Condition:

SalesTable[Region] = "North"
Step 3 — Validate Role
Modeling → View As Roles → Select:

North_Region
Your visuals should now show only North region records.

Example (Boxed)
+----------+-----------+
| Region   | Sales     |
+----------+-----------+
| North    | 15,000    |
| North    | 18,000    |
+----------+-----------+
(All other regions hidden)
Hint:
Static RLS is ideal when security rules do not depend on login identity.



TASK 2 — Implement Dynamic RLS (User-Specific Security)
Objective:
Restrict data based on the logged-in user using USERPRINCIPALNAME().

Step 1 — Create a Security Table
Create a table in Power BI:

SecurityTable
Columns:
UserEmail | AllowedRegion
Example:

rahul@abc.com | North
sita@abc.com  | South
Step 2 — Establish Relationship
Connect:

SecurityTable[AllowedRegion] → SalesTable[Region]
Relationship:

Many-to-One (Single)
Step 3 — Create Dynamic RLS Role
Modeling → Manage Roles → Create:

Role Name: Dynamic_RLS
Filter on SecurityTable:

SecurityTable[UserEmail] = USERPRINCIPALNAME()
Step 4 — Test Role
Modeling → View As → Other user:

rahul@abc.com
You should see only North region data.

Expected Result (Boxed)
Logged in as: sita@abc.com
Visible Region: South
All Other Regions: Hidden
Hint:
Dynamic RLS is used in enterprise dashboards where each user views only their assigned slice.

TASK 3 — Publishing & Assigning Security in Power BI Service
Objective:
Publish the report and map real users to RLS roles.



Step 1 — Publish Report
Home → Publish → Select workspace.

Step 2 — Assign Users to RLS Roles
Power BI Service → Workspace → Datasets

Click dataset → Security

Add users:

North_Region → rahul@abc.com
Dynamic_RLS → Add organization users
Step 3 — Validate Security Online
Dataset → Security → Test As Role

Choose:

rahul@abc.com
Dashboard updates based on RLS.

RLS Assignment Summary (Boxed)
+-------------------+---------------------------+ 
| Role              | Users                     |
+-------------------+---------------------------+
| North_Region      | rahul@abc.com             |
| Dynamic_RLS       | sita@abc.com, team leads  |
+-------------------+---------------------------+
Hint:
RLS works only when using a workspace with Pro or Premium licensing.

TASK 4 — Azure AD Integration with Power BI
Objective:
Use Azure Active Directory groups for scalable enterprise security.

Step 1 — Create Azure AD Security Group (Admin Step)
Azure Portal → Azure Active Directory → Groups → New Group

Enter:

Group Type: Security  
Group Name: PowerBI-North  
Members: Add relevant users
Step 2 — Map AAD Group to RLS Role
Go to:

Power BI Service → Dataset → Security

Assign:

Role: North_Region  
AAD Group: PowerBI-North
Step 3 — Test AAD-Based Security
“Test as role” → select AAD group.

Should display only North region.



Step 4 — Apply AAD for Dynamic RLS
Use:

SecurityTable[UserEmail] IN VALUES(SecurityAADGroup[Email])
Azure AD Integration Summary (Boxed)
+------------------------------+-----------------------------+
| Azure AD Group               | Power BI Role               |
+------------------------------+-----------------------------+
| PowerBI-North                | North_Region                |
| PowerBI-South                | South_Region                |
+------------------------------+-----------------------------+
Hint:
Using AAD groups avoids manually adding/removing users in Power BI roles.
